  "slug": "find-the-duplicate-number",
  "title": "Find the Duplicate Number",
  "topic": "arrays-hashing",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "hash set / repeated value scan",
  "statement": "You are given an integer array `nums`. A value counts as *repeated* when it appears at two different indices.\n\nReturn the smallest repeated value in `nums`. If no value is repeated, return `-1`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200` and `-100 <= nums[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-100]",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-5, 95, -59, 38, 99]",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Decide first what the function returns when nothing repeats, and pick a sentinel that cannot collide with a real array value.",
    "Grouping equal values is the whole job; the structure that groups them in a single left-to-right pass is a hash set of the values already visited.",
    "The first value you notice repeating is not necessarily the smallest one, so keep a running minimum instead of returning on the first hit."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force compares every pair of indices (i, j) with i < j, records nums[i] whenever nums[j] equals it, and keeps a running minimum in a slot initialised to null / None. That is O(n\u00b2) comparisons and no extra memory. The optimal pass keeps a hash set of the values met so far: a value seen for the first time goes into the set, a value already present is a repeat and is only compared against the current minimum. Every element is touched once, so the pass is O(n) time with O(n) set space.",
    "why_optimal": "Every element has to be looked at least once to learn whether it repeats, so \u03a9(n) time is unavoidable and the hash set reaches that bound; the extra set is the price of getting there in one pass. Since n <= 200 and every value lies in [-100, 100], a fixed 201-slot seen array would also work and would drop the space to O(1) in practice, but the set version does not depend on the value bound and survives a widening of the constraints.",
    "pitfalls": "Initialising the answer with -1 and using that same -1 as the 'nothing found yet' flag: -1 is a legal array value here, so for nums = [-1, 3, -1, 3] the comparison 'is the new repeat smaller than -1' never fires and the code reports 3. Use null / None or a separate boolean. Second, returning at the first repeat you meet is wrong because that value need not be the smallest one: nums = [5, 5, 1, 1] repeats 5 before 1. Third, the all-distinct array and the single element array must both answer -1, which is exactly the case an unset sentinel hides."
  },
